WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.100
And here is the range of the score, with what the different values mean in practice.

2
00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:13.700
Measures built for tertiary structures can be stretched to quaternary ones, by treating every chain in an assembly as a single long chain.

3
00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:21.950
The trouble is that you then cannot tell which part of the difference comes from the chains themselves and which from how they are placed relative to each other.

4
00:00:21.950 --> 00:00:34.750
So there are measures meant specifically for quaternary structure, and they divide into two cases: when you know which residue corresponds to which — two docking models built from the same monomers, say — and when you do not.

5
00:00:35.350 --> 00:00:37.350
Take the first case.

6
00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:40.725
The usual measure is ligand RMSD.

7
00:00:40.725 --> 00:00:46.200
The difference from ordinary RMSD is what you superimpose and what you measure.

8
00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:53.775
You superimpose on the receptors — the larger proteins — because even identical receptors may sit anywhere in space.

9
00:00:53.775 --> 00:01:00.825
That gives you a translation vector and a rotation matrix, and you apply those to the ligand, the smaller protein.

10
00:01:00.825 --> 00:01:07.025
Then you measure the RMSD between the transformed ligand and the ligand in the reference complex.

11
00:01:07.025 --> 00:01:12.050
That is ligand RMSD: aligned on the receptor, measured on the ligand.

12
00:01:12.650 --> 00:01:19.950
If you keep only the interface residues of the reference structure and measure over those, you get interface RMSD.

13
00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:23.325
The outline of the calculation is on the slide.

14
00:01:23.925 --> 00:01:31.650
There are also measures that need no superposition at all: the fraction of native contacts and the fraction of non-native contacts.

15
00:01:31.650 --> 00:01:35.625
One thing to notice is that the two do not add up to one.

16
00:01:35.625 --> 00:01:40.875
The denominators are different, which is counterintuitive the first time you meet it.

17
00:01:40.875 --> 00:01:46.300
At the bottom of the slide is how those three quantities together classify a comparison.

18
00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:50.275
High quality means the two structures are nearly identical.

19
00:01:50.275 --> 00:01:54.925
Incorrect means the ligand is in quite the wrong place against the receptor.

20
00:01:55.525 --> 00:02:03.750
Finally the DockQ score, which takes the rather complicated CAPRI criteria and combines them empirically into a single number.

21
00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:10.375
It is here because you will meet it again when the course discusses AI-based modelling of protein complexes.

22
00:02:10.975 --> 00:02:18.425
Now the harder case: complexes made of different proteins, where there is no obvious correspondence between residues.

23
00:02:18.425 --> 00:02:21.900
The two examples on screen are of that kind.

24
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:26.900
The trick is to stop comparing atoms and compare a frame instead.

25
00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:33.425
Each complex is represented by fourteen points: seven for the receptor and seven for the ligand.

26
00:02:33.425 --> 00:02:36.975
The first point of each set is the centre of mass.

27
00:02:36.975 --> 00:02:47.625
The other six sit a few ångström away along the x, y and z directions — their coordinates come straight from the centre of mass by adding or subtracting a fixed amount.

28
00:02:48.225 --> 00:02:54.925
Superimpose the second receptor onto the first, and those fourteen points land in particular positions.

29
00:02:54.925 --> 00:02:58.450
Those are the positions marked with the subscript R.

30
00:02:59.050 --> 00:03:03.250
Now superimpose the second ligand onto the first instead.

31
00:03:03.250 --> 00:03:06.475
The same fourteen points land somewhere else.

32
00:03:06.475 --> 00:03:09.900
Those are the positions marked with the subscript L.

33
00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:22.075
The interaction RMSD is measured between those two sets of positions: the fourteen points as the receptor alignment puts them, against the fourteen points as the ligand alignment puts them.

34
00:03:22.675 --> 00:03:30.825
And this is how interaction RMSD relates to ligand and interface RMSD, measured on a limited set of CAPRI targets.

35
00:03:30.825 --> 00:03:38.250
The correlation is good, which is the point: a measure that needs no residue correspondence still tracks the ones that do.

36
00:03:38.850 --> 00:03:40.825
Now to prediction.

37
00:03:40.825 --> 00:03:52.550
The Protein Data Bank may look large, but the number of proteins that exist is vastly larger, and the prevailing view is that what a protein does is largely set by its three-dimensional structure.

38
00:03:52.550 --> 00:03:56.375
So the structures that elude experiment have to be modelled.

39
00:03:56.375 --> 00:04:02.800
For a long time, methods for modelling a single protein came in two kinds, shown at the top of the slide.

40
00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:12.275
Around two thousand a third appeared, predicting physical contacts between amino acids from evolutionary information in a multiple sequence alignment.

41
00:04:12.275 --> 00:04:20.175
And in twenty twenty the AI-based methods arrived, predicting tertiary structure with an accuracy nobody had seen before.

42
00:04:20.175 --> 00:04:28.775
That led to claims that the problem is solved, and the AlphaFold database now holds something like six hundred million models of single proteins.

43
00:04:28.775 --> 00:04:32.200
This lecture covers the methods that came before.

44
00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:43.600
Two reasons they are still worth knowing: the contact-based methods are an essential part of the AI pipelines, and all three older approaches are built into those pipelines in one form or another.

45
00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:48.075
Understanding them sketchily helps in understanding what came after.

46
00:04:48.675 --> 00:04:56.650
This is ab initio modelling in outline: build the structure from the sequence and from physical principles, with no example to copy.

47
00:04:57.250 --> 00:05:04.475
And this is template-based modelling, which uses structures already determined for proteins with similar sequences.

48
00:05:04.475 --> 00:05:14.975
Textbooks usually split this in two — homology modelling when the target and template sequences are homologous, threading when they are merely similar — but here they are treated as one thing.

49
00:05:15.575 --> 00:05:20.275
Before methods for quaternary structure, a word about vocabulary.

50
00:05:20.275 --> 00:05:27.075
"Predicting protein–protein interactions" is used loosely and means two different things, as the slide shows.

51
00:05:27.075 --> 00:05:30.450
One is whether two proteins interact at all.

52
00:05:30.450 --> 00:05:35.300
The other is how they interact — the three-dimensional structure of the complex.

53
00:05:35.300 --> 00:05:40.075
This lecture is about the second, which is usually called protein docking.

54
00:05:40.675 --> 00:05:46.525
Docking methods divide the same three ways: template-free, template-based, and deep learning.

55
00:05:46.525 --> 00:05:49.650
There is one difference from the tertiary case.

56
00:05:49.650 --> 00:05:57.900
For complexes, the similarity between target and template can be assessed on two levels, sequence and structure, not just sequence.

57
00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:09.550
In template-free docking you start from the structures of the two proteins, generate as many candidate arrangements as you can — decoys — and then pick one or a few as the final models.

58
00:06:10.150 --> 00:06:14.300
And this is the assumption that template-based docking rests on.

59
00:06:14.900 --> 00:06:19.975
Here is the algorithm for template-based docking using sequence similarity.

60
00:06:19.975 --> 00:06:25.625
Notice what it does: it models the tertiary and quaternary structures at the same time.

61
00:06:25.625 --> 00:06:28.475
This is the fold-and-dock approach.

62
00:06:29.075 --> 00:06:33.850
And this is template-based docking using structure similarity instead.

63
00:06:33.850 --> 00:06:41.900
Here the two are separate — fold first, then dock — so modelling the individual structures and modelling the complex are independent of each other.

64
00:06:42.500 --> 00:06:49.350
The last part of the lecture is about classifying structures, and the databases that hold the classifications.

65
00:06:49.350 --> 00:06:52.225
First, though, what a protein domain is.

66
00:06:52.825 --> 00:06:55.500
And what a structural motif is.

67
00:06:56.100 --> 00:06:59.600
This is the classification scheme in common use.

68
00:07:00.200 --> 00:07:03.050
These are the older databases.

69
00:07:03.050 --> 00:07:10.250
They are no longer the first place to look, but they come up constantly in the literature, so it is worth knowing the names.

70
00:07:10.850 --> 00:07:18.825
And these are the newer ones, covering experimentally determined structures, with a recent extension to AlphaFold models.

71
00:07:19.425 --> 00:07:23.675
This is the classification scheme that newer database uses.

72
00:07:24.275 --> 00:07:33.100
That is the lecture: from atoms to proteins, how to measure whether two structures are alike, how structures are predicted, and how they are classified.

